  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If $U=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${U}^{3}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, to find ${U}^{2},$ we multiply $U$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${U}^{3}={U}^{2}\\cdot U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 24 \\\\ -24 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
